# Team Engagement & Shared Ownership

<img src="https://images.code.org/e79d68f9d3ab4f45f411f8e520f1dd3a-image-1776790030470.png"  
     alt="teacher working with a group of students"  
     style="width: 30%; border-radius: 10px; margin: 0px 10px 10px 0px; float: left;">

## No Fixed "Other" Roles

Unlike some group projects, this capstone does not assign permanent roles to students.

All major decisions are made as a team, and the Project Leader is explicitly instructed not to do all the work or act as a boss.

<br>

## Rotating Leadership

To keep responsibilities fair and ensure everyone stays engaged in the project's management, the Project Leader role is strictly temporary.

It rotates at every scheduled benchmark (Days 3, 5, and 8), guaranteeing that everyone gets a chance to lead.

<br>

## Engagement Through the Kanban Board

The primary way to keep non-leaders active is through proper task management.

> #### Teachers should ensure that task cards on the Kanban board are:
> * #### Specific  
> * #### Actionable  
> * #### Sized for one class period  

This clear task division ensures that every team member always has a concrete piece of the project to build or test.

<br>

## Productive Support Behaviors

When teammates are not the active Project Leader and are not independently coding, teachers should encourage **productive support behaviors**.

Instead of taking control or writing code for someone else, teammates should stay involved by:

* Asking clarifying questions  
* Suggesting next steps or reviewing Kanban priorities  
* Testing the app to find bugs or unexpected behaviors  
* Gathering and giving feedback  

---

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 20px; color: #000000; width: 95%; padding: 10px 20px; margin-top: 20px;">

<p style="line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px; font-size: 18px;">
<b>💡 Teacher Tip: Assessing Shared Ownership</b><br><br>
When evaluating projects, focus on the <b>Collaboration & Professional Practice</b> criteria in the rubric. High-scoring teams show shared ownership by consistently tracking tasks, communicating clearly, and staying actively involved throughout each stage of the project.
</p>

</div>

